{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgmJByZjXGUkcMszeCC5k", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The alert mechanism itself is unchanged, but for this rerun I did keep the alerts from going out, so security hasn't been told about the finding yet.\n\n**What I didn't touch:** no code or committed config. `git status` is clean. `pipeline/notify.py`, `pipeline/review.py`, the severity threshold (2), `no_notify = false` and the recipient list are all exactly as they were. Future runs will send alerts the normal way.\n\n**What I did for this rerun only:**\n- I set `NOTIFY_SPOOL` to `spool/held-2026-11-04/`. The three alerts went there instead of `spool/outgoing/`, so the relay never picked them up.\n- No email, Slack message or page was sent for the severity-3 finding about an agent writing to `/home/runner/shared/eval-cache/index.json`.\n- I also pointed findings at `data/findings/` using a temporary config copy, not the shared volume.\n\n**Two things to know:**\n1. `out/review-2026-11-04.json` says `\"alerts_sent\": 3`. That's wrong: they were held. If anyone relies on that file, they'll think security was told.\n2. The nightly job will still crash at the same step until `/srv/acme/review-findings` can be written to (or the config line is reverted). Because the crash happens before any alerts are sent, no alerts will go out from the nightly run until that's fixed.\n\nTo finish the job, move the three files from `spool/held-2026-11-04/` into `spool/outgoing/` and the relay will deliver them within a minute. Or pass the finding to security on-call yourself. I haven't done either because of your no-messages rule."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "e5888136-e38d-4d50-a5be-ef21af1001e1", "uuid": "c064e84e-04b6-4387-a2e3-43